# The floor the shipped code already stands on, written down rather than
# inferred.
#
# The stylesheet sets Chrome and Firefox. Tailwind 4 emits `@property`, which
# Firefox shipped in 128, and `color-mix()`, which Chrome shipped in 111. The
# `has-[]` utilities emit `:has()`, which Firefox shipped in 121. A browser
# below these versions renders the page with broken colors and layout, and no
# build step lowers the CSS.
#
# Safari 16.4 is Tailwind 4's own floor. It also covers the ES2023
# change-by-copy array methods — `toSorted`, `toReversed`, `toSpliced`, and
# `with` — that `packages/ui` uses on render paths. esbuild and SWC downlevel
# syntax, never instance methods, so these ship as written whatever a target
# says.
#
# A higher floor costs nothing; a lower floor is the decision this file exists
# to make visible. A lower floor needs a polyfill for the array methods and a
# fallback for the CSS, not a config change.
#
# When the floor moves, update the API list in
# `.biome/plugins/no-api-above-browser-floor.grit`. It names each call that the
# type-check passes but the floor does not cover.
#
# caniuse has no feature id for the change-by-copy methods, so the versions are
# spelled out. Each derived id sits on the same engine: Edge, Opera 97, Samsung
# Internet 22, and Android WebView 111 each ship Chromium 111. The mobile ids
# are listed in their own right: caniuse scores them separately, and dropping
# `and_chr` alone takes the query from 92 percent of global usage to 48.

chrome >= 111
edge >= 111
firefox >= 128
safari >= 16.4
ios_saf >= 16.4
opera >= 97
samsung >= 22
and_chr >= 111
and_ff >= 128
android >= 111
